{{define "server-help-mcp"}}
{{template "public" .}}
{{end}}

{{define "content"}}
<div class="content">
    <article class="prose mcp-help">
        <h1>MCP server</h1>

        <section id="overview">
            <h2>What this is</h2>
            <p>{{.AppName}} exposes its public API operations as tools over remote Streamable HTTP. An assistant can call those tools through the same API used by other clients.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="endpoint">
            <h2>Endpoint</h2>
            <p>Use the canonical endpoint or either direct alias. Select the address field and copy it in your browser.</p>
            <label for="mcp-endpoint">Canonical URL</label>
            <input id="mcp-endpoint" class="mcp-endpoint" type="text" readonly value="{{.MCPURL}}" aria-label="MCP endpoint URL">
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy="{{.MCPURL}}" aria-label="Copy MCP endpoint"><span class="copy-icon">📋</span><span class="copy-text" aria-live="polite">Copy</span></button>
            <ul>
                <li>Alias: <code>{{.MCPAPIURL}}</code></li>
                <li>Alias: <code>{{.MCPAliasURL}}</code></li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="authentication">
            <h2>Authentication</h2>
            <p>Use the same <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> tokens as the REST API. Anonymous clients can use public tools. For ongoing automation, create a linked token for this client at <a href="{{ path "/server/preferences/tokens" }}">Preferences → Tokens</a>, so you can revoke it independently. Do not use your primary token or the operator token.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="connect">
            <h2>Connect a client</h2>
            <h3>Claude Code</h3>
            <pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http {{.MCPProjectName}} {{.MCPURL}} --header "Authorization: Bearer $MCP_TOKEN"</code></pre>
            <h3>Generic JSON client configuration</h3>
            <pre><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{.MCPProjectName}}": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "{{.MCPURL}}",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer $MCP_TOKEN"
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
            <h3>Verify with curl</h3>
            <pre><code>curl -X POST '{{.MCPURL}}' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer '"$MCP_TOKEN" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-11-25","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"mcp-check","version":"1.0"}}}'</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="tools">
            <h2>Tools available to anonymous callers</h2>
            <p>This live list is generated from the same OpenAPI operation registry used by MCP. Authenticated callers may see additional tools allowed by their token.</p>
            {{if .MCPTools}}
            <ul>
                {{range .MCPTools}}<li><code>{{.Name}}</code> — {{.Description}}</li>{{end}}
            </ul>
            {{else}}
            <p>No anonymous tools are currently available.</p>
            {{end}}
        </section>

        <section id="limits">
            <h2>Behavior and limits</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Requests use HTTP POST with JSON-RPC; the server is stateless and does not create sessions.</li>
                <li>MCP calls use the API's rate limits, validation, ownership checks, and audit path.</li>
                <li>Browser origins are checked to prevent cross-origin and DNS-rebinding requests.</li>
                <li>Supported protocol versions are 2024-11-05, 2025-03-26, 2025-06-18, 2025-11-25, and 2026-07-28. The server negotiates the version requested by the client.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="troubleshooting">
            <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Cause</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>400</code></td><td>Unsupported protocol version or mismatched protocol headers</td><td>Use a supported protocol version and let your MCP client send headers matching its JSON-RPC request.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Missing, invalid, expired, or revoked token</td><td>Check the Bearer token and create or restore a token on <a href="{{ path "/server/preferences/tokens" }}">Preferences → Tokens</a>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>403</code></td><td>Origin rejected or token lacks the required owner access</td><td>Connect from the expected origin and use a token authorized for the requested resource.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>MCP is disabled</td><td>Ask the server operator to enable <code>server.mcp.enabled</code> in <code>server.yml</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>405</code></td><td>Unsupported HTTP method</td><td>Send MCP requests with POST; GET and DELETE are not supported.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section id="project">
            <h2>VidVeil tools</h2>
            {{.MCPProject | markdownToHTML}}
        </section>
    </article>
</div>
{{end}}
